    <article class="doc">
        <h2 id="use">Using services</h2>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Command</th>
                    <th>Purpose</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-service name start</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Start immediately.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-service name stop</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Stop immediately.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-service name restart</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Restart immediately.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-service name reload</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Ask the service to reload configuration.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-service name status</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Show runtime state.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-status</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Show current runlevel state.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>rc-status -a</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Show all runlevels.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            Runtime actions do not enable a service for the next boot. Use
            <code>rc-update</code> for persistent runlevel membership.
        </p>

        <h2 id="runlevels">Runlevels</h2>

        <pre><code>rc-update add dbus default
rc-update del dbus default
rc-update show
rc-update show boot
rc-status --list</code></pre>

        <p>
            Vylen uses the conventional <code>sysinit</code>, <code>boot</code>,
            <code>default</code>, and <code>shutdown</code> runlevels.
            Dependencies are started before a service and stopped in the reverse
            relationship.
        </p>

        <h2 id="write">Writing a service</h2>

        <p>
            Place an executable script at <code>/etc/init.d/example</code>. Use
            the OpenRC interpreter and declare a supervised command.
        </p>

        <pre><code>#!/sbin/openrc-run
name="example"
description="Example service"
command="/usr/bin/exampled"
command_args="--config /etc/example/config.toml"
command_user="example:example"
pidfile="/run/${RC_SVCNAME}.pid"

depend() {
    need net
    use dns logger
    after firewall
}

start_pre() {
    checkpath \
        --directory \
        --owner "${command_user}" /run/example
}</code></pre>

        <pre><code>chmod 0755 /etc/init.d/example
rc-service example start
rc-service example status
rc-update add example default</code></pre>

        <h2 id="dependencies">Dependencies and supervision</h2>

        <p>
            Use <code>need</code> for a hard prerequisite, <code>use</code> for
            an optional service, <code>after</code> for ordering without a hard
            dependency, and <code>before</code> to place the service earlier.
            Prefer <code>command</code>, <code>command_args</code>,
            <code>command_user</code>, and <code>pidfile</code> over custom
            daemon loops.
        </p>

        <h2 id="debug">Debugging</h2>

        <pre><code>rc-service example status
rc-status -a
rc-update show default
rc-service --debug example restart
dmesg | tail -n 50</code></pre>

        <p>
            Check executable permissions, absolute paths, ownership of runtime
            directories, missing dependencies, and stale pidfiles. Keep
            configuration in <code>/etc</code>, runtime state in
            <code>/run</code>, and enable a service only after a successful manual start.
        </p>
    </article>
